1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:05,510
You tap send. But where does your message actually go?

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,200
Your phone turns it into radio waves, and beams them to the nearest cell tower.

3
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:17,100
The tower sends it underground, as flashes of light inside a glass fiber.

4
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:22,600
At your carrier's core network, a computer looks up where your friend's phone was last seen.

5
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:27,380
If their phone is off, the text waits here until it wakes up.

6
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:33,190
Then it's sent to the towers nearby, which call out to their phone by radio.

7
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:38,800
A phone call takes the same road. But a call can't wait.

8
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,680
Your voice is cut into tiny packets, 50 every second, flowing both ways at once.

9
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:55,000
Radio, light, and one quick lookup, for millions of people, every second of the day.
